  "report": {
    "incident_title": "Login down for many customers - looks like a network/ISP outage",
    "outcome": "conclusive",
    "summary": "Login outage is caused by an internal configuration change, not Cloudflare or an ISP. At 11:40Z, PR #874 changed auth-svc's AUTH_DB_POOL_MAX from 50 to 5. The PR described the change as a minimum-idle tune. auth-svc's HikariCP pool is now capped at 5 connections. Login traffic (roughly 40 to 220 rps over the window, which is partly a retry pattern - an assumption) saturates those 5 connections. Requests queue behind the pool, p99 climbs to the 30s timeout, and logins hang. The Cloudflare notice covers only the S\u00e3o Paulo (GRU) data center, and origin packet loss and infra events show no network fault. The reporter's Cloudflare/ISP explanation is not supported. Engineering should not post a Cloudflare attribution. The mitigation is to restore AUTH_DB_POOL_MAX to 50 (previous value) and confirm pool recovery.",
    "root_cause": "Config change PR #874 (identity-team, 11:40Z) lowered auth-svc AUTH_DB_POOL_MAX from 50 to 5 instead of setting minimum idle. The HikariCP pool (max=5) starved under normal login load, which produced queueing and 30s connection timeouts on POST /login.",
